<section>
<p>Chapter one · The edge of town</p>
<p><strong>A slower kind of looking</strong></p>
<p>These field notes are an invitation to read a landscape at walking pace. Begin with a small place, an open afternoon, and a little curiosity.</p>
<p>The path begins where the last houses end. At first there are small sounds: a gate closing, a bicycle resting against a wall, the wind passing through dry grass. A little farther on, each sound has room around it.</p>
<p>We keep a notebook for things that are easy to overlook. The shape of a leaf is useful, but so is the way its shadow falls at four in the afternoon. Observation becomes easier when we stop trying to name everything at once.</p>
<p>Walking slowly changes the scale of a familiar place. What looked like a green bank from the road becomes a hillside of grasses, flowers, stones, and old roots. Every few steps offer another beginning.</p>
<p><strong>Small observations</strong></p>
